<p>When the damage overlaps, each adjuster may point at the other policy. A clear account of what caused which damage, room by room, helps keep the claims from falling into the gap between them. Our article on <a href="/wind-vs-flood-damage-port-arthur-insurance/">wind vs. flood damage</a> explains how that distinction is usually drawn, and our <a href="/services/twia-windstorm-claims/">TWIA windstorm claims</a> page covers the wind side in detail.</p>

<h2>How Port Arthur Homes Are Built Affects the Claim</h2>
<p>Local construction shapes both the damage and the way it should be estimated.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Slab-on-grade homes</strong> are common in newer subdivisions. Water that reaches the floor tends to wick into drywall and baseboards quickly.</li>
<li><strong>Pier-and-beam homes</strong> in many older neighborhoods have crawlspaces where moisture, damaged insulation and subfloor issues can go unnoticed.</li>
<li><strong>Composition shingle roofs</strong> dominate. Wind can crease and unseal shingles without tearing them off, which is easy to miss from the ground.</li>
<li><strong>Brick veneer, vinyl and fiber-cement siding</strong> each fail differently in high wind and wind-driven rain.</li>
<li><strong>Older housing stock</strong> may have materials that are no longer made, raising matching and replacement questions.</li>
</ul>
<p>High humidity also means water left in walls can lead to mold within days, which is why prompt drying and documentation matter. See our <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a> page for more.</p>

