<h2>Rental Homes, Condos and Older Family Properties</h2>
<p>Landlords in Port Arthur often own older homes insured on dwelling or landlord forms. Those forms may cover the building and loss of rents but treat contents and code upgrades differently from a homeowners policy. Condo and townhome owners have yet another layer, because the association’s master policy may cover some of the structure while the owner’s unit policy covers the rest. In each case, the starting point is reading the actual policy form, not assuming it works like a neighbor’s.</p>

<h2>A Typical Residential Claim, Step by Step</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Report and protect.</strong> Notify every insurer that may be involved, then tarp, board up and dry what you safely can.</li>
<li><strong>Document before cleanup.</strong> Photograph and video each room, the roof, the yard and damaged belongings before anything is thrown away. Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> helps.</li>
<li><strong>Inspection.</strong> The insurer’s adjuster inspects. Being present, with your own notes and photos, helps make sure nothing is skipped.</li>
<li><strong>Estimate and payment.</strong> The insurer issues an estimate and a first payment, often at actual cash value under replacement cost policies.</li>
<li><strong>Repairs and supplements.</strong> Hidden damage found during repairs is added through a supplement, and withheld depreciation is requested once work is finished.</li>
</ol>
<p>Problems at any of these stages are where claims tend to go off track, whether that is an incomplete inspection, a low estimate or a claim that simply stops moving.</p>

<h2>What Repairs in Port Arthur May Require</h2>
<p>The City of Port Arthur Building Inspections Division operates under the 2021 International Residential Code, and re-roofs and structural repairs generally need a permit. In Jefferson County, roof repairs and re-roofs must also meet TDI windstorm requirements to maintain TWIA eligibility, documented with a WPI-8 certificate. Those requirements can add cost. Whether the insurer pays for it depends on your ordinance or law coverage. Our <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a> page has details and contact information for the city.</p>

<h2>How a Port Arthur Public Adjuster Handles Residential Claims</h2>
<p>A Port Arthur Public Adjuster works for the policyholder, not the insurance company. On a residential claim, that usually means:</p>
<ul>
<li>Reviewing every policy on the home, including endorsements and deductibles</li>
<li>Inspecting the roof, exterior, interior, attic and crawlspace</li>
<li>Photographing and measuring the damage, often with moisture meters and thermal imaging</li>
<li>Preparing a detailed Xactimate estimate and a contents inventory</li>
<li>Communicating with each insurer and tracking every deadline</li>
</ul>
<p>We do not perform or arrange repairs on claims we adjust; Texas Insurance Code Chapter 4102 prohibits that conflict. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. If you are unsure whether you need help at all, our article on <a href="/public-adjuster-pros-and-cons/">public adjuster pros and cons</a> lays out both sides, or you can request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a>.</p>
